```

When this document and observed KSP behavior disagree, record the discrepancy. Adjust adapter implementation without changing player-facing behavior. A player-facing change requires an explicit amendment. Closed decisions do not require repeated questions; genuinely unresolved API feasibility must be reported.

### 0.1 Revision 5 closed implementation decisions

This revision integrates the logistics review into the normative system rules, not a separate optional wish list. Keep the stock-fuel/depot design and finite starter kit. Add a baseline Metal-only battery bank; retain the advanced battery reward. Use one reservation authority, persisted dispatch policies, separate handling berths and fleet parking, a bounded wait-graph diagnostic, network bottleneck feedback, and a complete gameplay scenario. The surface deck remains fixed: expansion means upgrades or separately anchored colonies connected by paid transport, not free extra decks. Reduce initial machine wear to the value in section 8; calibration must measure intervention frequency.

Existing phase numbers 0-21 remain stable; lettered phases are required intervening sessions, not optional suffixes. Do not implement the whole release in one session. This document defines requested behavior; it does not claim KSP adapter feasibility or game calibration has already passed.

## 1 Scope and closed decisions

### 1.1 Player loop

1. Accept a Lodeworks campaign contract at Mission Control.
2. Launch a hab and deploy it on a suitable landed site.
3. Place the hab's included starter buildings on its deck, with no loose construction cargo requirement.
4. Use local production to pay material costs for extra storage, buildings and upgrades.
5. Inspect the deposit; mine ore into graded batches and optionally process it.
6. Establish manual shipments or automated routes to other colonies or KSC.
7. Supply enough qualifying cargo to the contract's receiving endpoint.
8. Complete the contract and receive funds, reputation, and permanent blueprints.
9. Use larger storage, faster equipment, and more capable transports to expand the network.

Interesting constraints are extraction rate, grade, deposit life, power, storage, fuel production, propellant mass, vehicle range, depot placement, transport capacity, journey time, maintenance, and campaign objectives. Avoid repetitive colony switching as a requirement to keep ordinary operations running.

### 1.2 Mandatory decisions

- One colony has one deployed hab part as its physical anchor. Additional buildings and shipment vehicles are cosmetic GameObjects, not vessels.
- Colony and depot cargo and transport propellants live only in the scenario-owned ledger. Do not mirror them into stock resource tanks. Explicit manual stock-fuel bridge transfers are allowed under section 9.6, with exactly one inventory owner before and after each transfer.
- Colonies use their own explicit power system, specified in section 7. Colony power is not stock ElectricCharge. This intentionally replaces the old unloaded-EC restriction and RequestResource factory implementation.
- Stock solar panels, batteries, fuel cells, ISRU, and stock Ore remain unchanged. They operate normally on spacecraft and do not silently power colony machinery. Transport uses the original stock LiquidFuel/Oxidizer IDs and their stock metadata, never a custom finished propellant; fuel-feed is only a raw production ingredient.
- Shipment cargo leaves source inventory only when a departure transaction succeeds. Animations never own cargo or change arrival time.
- Automated routes are authoritative gameplay. Cosmetic trucks remain pictures of local activity; local transfers are instantaneous.
- First deployment has one finite prefabricated starter set included with each hab. Extra buildings and upgrades consume the endpoint's Metal and, where specified, Machine Parts. No ongoing building placement funds fee substitutes for these materials. All advanced construction needs its blueprint; supplied starter pieces are baseline exceptions defined explicitly below. Funds still pay real parts, vehicle commissioning, transport services and emergency repairs.
- The campaign uses deliveries and event counters, never inventory subtraction from acceptance-time snapshots.
- KSC is a permanent abstract receiving endpoint, not a vessel or a colony. It provides neither remote mining nor an unlimited remote power source. KSC may sell fuel to a transport physically present there, through an explicit local purchase; it cannot remotely refill colonies or depots.
- Preserve ore provenance through mining, stacking, processing selection, shipments, and quest validation.
- No required life support, radiation, city population, physical traffic, terrain flattening, or multiplayer.
- No kOS, kRPC, Kerbalism, MKS, Kopernicus, or Kerbal Konstructs dependency. Unknown celestial bodies receive a default profile.
- Orbital depots are in this release. The player launches a real depot-core part on an ordinary stock vessel and registers a valid orbit. Cargo transport models remain cosmetic. Stock vessel construction is deferred.

### 1.3 Modes

Career is the reference campaign mode. Science mode has campaign blueprints and contracts only if the verified stock contract integration supports it; otherwise use the same quest records through the campaign window, with no funds or reputation transaction. Sandbox has every blueprint available by default and zero funds costs, while power, inventory, material construction costs, transport, and optional wear still operate. Sandbox also supplies the same starter kit; optional debug grants are explicit and campaign-ineligible. Offer a sandbox campaign challenge toggle using the campaign window, without pretending Mission Control exists there.

## 2 Architecture and ownership

Use C# 9, net48 for the KSP plugin, netstandard2.0 for the pure simulation, and net8.0 for tests. Verify target runtime compatibility before building features.

```text
Lodeworks.Sim
  InventoryMath, ReservationBook, AllocationPolicy, RecipeMath, ExtractionMath, PowerMath, WearMath, FaultMath
  FuelMath, ManeuverBudget, TransportPlanner, DepotSimulator
  ShipmentMath, RouteScheduler, DispatchPolicy, BerthScheduler, WaitGraph
  NetworkDiagnostics, NetworkForecast, ColonySimulator, WorldSimulator
  CampaignEvaluator, deterministic hashes, config validation, schema migration
Lodeworks.Ksp
  LodeworksScenario, LodeworksHab, LodeworksDepotCore, endpoint discovery, contracts
  BlueprintAdapter, celestial environment adapter, UI, visuals, save adapter
GameData/Lodeworks
  Parts, Resources, Recipes, Buildings, BodyProfiles, Campaign, Transport
  Localization, Icons, Settings, version metadata
```

Ship both Lodeworks.dll and Lodeworks.Sim.dll. Never ship KSP/Unity assemblies or test dependencies. Pin build dependencies and record their versions. KSPBuildTools is a build dependency only; verify its packaging behavior for both output assemblies.

The scenario owns colonies, orbital depots, buildings, batches, veins, fuel-feed reservoirs, routes, located vehicles and their onboard propellant, shipments, campaign progress, unlocks, and simulation clocks. The hab persists only anchor identity and deploy state needed to reconnect to scenario records. Never have competing converter state on both a PartModule and a building record.

KSP integration gathers validated environment and crew snapshots, advances the pure simulation, and presents resulting state. UI commands use the same transaction methods as automated commands. Never modify inventory directly from IMGUI drawing or an animation callback.

Use stable colony, building, route, depot, vehicle, transport-slot, shipment, contract-instance, and event IDs. Vessel GUIDs are references, not colony identity. Use invariant serialization and deterministic ordering by stable ID.

## 3 Colonies and placement

### 3.1 Deployment

The hab is lw-hab-250. It has no crew seats; a stock crewed part attached to the same vessel supplies crew. Crew efficiency is applied to processing and extraction, not autonomous transport or unmanned depot transfer operations.

Each hab carries a persisted starter-kit inventory and immutable grant receipt tied to the actual part identity. This is prefabricated equipment, not seed ore or free fuel. Grant once per newly launched hab after valid deployment, never once per scene/colony ID; moving or redeploying the same hab carries its remaining kit state rather than resetting it. A newly launched replacement hab is a genuinely new paid part and may have its own kit.

Create a colony only after an explicit Deploy Colony action on a landed vessel. Refuse prelaunch, orbiting, flying, splashed, Sun, and terrainless-body deployment. Show the actual reason. Do not award seed inventory in the shipped configuration.

Measure terrain and validate placement before committing the colony. Deployment cannot first create inventory and later discover that the site is invalid. Store bodyName, latitude, normalized longitude, heading, anchor part identity, and colonyId. The cell and surveyed site remain fixed after founding.

Allow one deployed colony anchor per vessel. Refuse deployment of a second anchor on that vessel. If docking joins two already deployed colonies, suspend industry and transport departures for both until separation restores valid anchors; do not merge or delete ledgers. Remap vessel references using anchor part identity after docking/undocking.

If an anchor is no longer landed or moves more than 20 m from its saved site, suspend extraction, production, and departure. Preserve inventory. Resuming requires return within 20 m and a valid landed state, or explicit relocation. A pushed hab cannot mine the previous cell from elsewhere.

### 3.2 Deck

Use a 24 m square deck with a 5 by 5 placement grid; grid indices -2 through 2, spacing 4 m. The hab occupies 0,0. One building per remaining slot. Cosmetic models must fit their footprint. A transport terminal occupies a grid slot and contains the pad visual; departing models do not require an additional vessel.

Convert corner offsets to geographic coordinates using stored heading. Raycast terrain first, excluding vessels and Lodeworks objects; use a verified PQS height query as fallback. Retry up to ten frames after load for missing terrain colliders. Place deck altitude 1.5 m above the highest corner. Reject new sites with corner height difference over 8 m or atan(heightDifference / 24 m) over 15 degrees. Cap visible legs at 8 m. Never reject an existing save merely because terrain LOD changes.

Parent visual roots to the anchor and calculate their local pose from saved geographic placement. Account for vessel orientation; parenting alone does not keep a deck level if the hab tilts. Rebuild after load, and destroy visuals when packed/unloaded. Do not store raw world position or edit PQS.

### 3.3 Placement from the hab

Open the hab PAW and select Open Lodeworks -> Buildings. The catalog has Included kit, Build more and Upgrade controls. Select a building, display a deck-local ghost at the hovered grid cell, rotate where useful, and click to place. Preview occupied footprint, availability, operating draw, rate/capacity and cost. Invalid placement is red with its actual reason. Escape/cancel changes nothing. Keyboard/controller alternatives may select a cell by dropdown; no raw world-coordinate entry.

Included kit consumes a stored prefab token and installs a complete Tier 0 building immediately. It does not consume Metal/Parts. Build more reserves all listed materials from the local ledger and starts a 30 UT-second construction job using 2 colony power/s; Upgrade uses its incremental recipe and a 60 UT-second job. One automated builder job runs per endpoint; no Kerbal EVA clicking or loaded-scene waiting is required. Pause on no power or invalid anchor. Integrated solar/generator output still follows power simulation. Building jobs join the same chronological simulator and are persisted.

Advance to command UT, validate blueprint, kit/material availability, grid, storage and anchor, then commit atomically. A failed command creates no row, consumes no token/resources and pays no fee. Materials reserved for construction cannot also be shipped or used for maintenance. An unfinished footprint remains occupied but adds no production/storage capability until completion. Its construction materials remain accounted in a job escrow. Cancel before completion restores all reserved materials if capacity permits, otherwise refuse cancellation until space is made; no partial phantom refund.

Move a completed building within the deck for free. Upgrade replaces its stats in the same grid slot, preserving condition, recipe/batch selection, machine identity and fault/repair state; it does not restore condition to 1 for free. Do not upgrade while its repair/transport job is active. New maximum rate/capacity applies at completion; old production machine is stopped during upgrade. Storage retains its old capacity until the upgraded capacity commits; an unfinished upgrade cannot hide already stored goods or release reserved cargo space. Capacity upgrades cannot create inventory or propellant.

### 3.4 Included starter set

The hab includes its built-in basic smelter, owned 20 power/s starter generator, 2000-unit battery and small integrated storage. These require no extra placement. Its separate included kit has exactly one of each:

| Included placeable | Count | Purpose |
|---|---:|---|
| Basic ore drill | 1 | Mine graded ore |
| Fuel-feed pump | 1 | Obtain refinery feed without consuming all campaign ore |
| Basic refinery | 1 | Make stock LiquidFuel/Oxidizer from ore or feed |
| Basic transport terminal | 1 | Handle surface cargo and later fueled launches |
| Ore bin | 1 | Store mined batches |
| Fuel-feed bin | 1 | Buffer raw feed |
| Metal bin | 1 | Store expansion materials |
| LiquidFuel tank | 1 | Store the stock fuel component |
| Oxidizer tank | 1 | Store the stock oxidizer component |

These occupy nine of the available 24 deck cells when all placed. Placement order is the player's choice. A tutorial suggests ore drill -> ore bin -> Metal bin -> refinery/pump/feed bin -> separate fuel tanks -> terminal. A starter terminal may be placed before c01, but its transport services stay locked until the campaign reward. Baseline capabilities must allow production and manufacture of extra basic storage before any advanced blueprint.

Free starter equipment cannot be sold for funds or salvaged into manufactured material. Dismantling a completed starter piece returns its original token, never a new token plus Metal. The piece's condition/fault and identity stay on that token; replacing a worn drill from the same token cannot repair it for free. Upgraded starter pieces return a packed piece at their current tier/state, keeping the upgrade's material investment; they cannot repeatedly refund it while retaining an upgraded prefab. Extra material-built pieces salvage 50% of actually paid Metal/Parts at completed dismantle, never more than the original paid recipe. Show and confirm permanent loss of the remaining material. No refunds for debug-granted equipment.

A hab variant's built-in terminal occupies no deck slot and fulfills the starter terminal entitlement: mark that token installed-in-hab rather than granting a duplicate free terminal. Variants improve their integrated equipment and storage through their explicit profiles, not by receiving multiple starter sets. Initial part prices/masses include their prefabricated kit; record kit mass/cost in the part config and do not mint another paid component on every deployment. Detailed surface-ledger ground-facility mass remains an abstraction, but the player must launch the actual kit-bearing hab.

### 3.5 Expansion and dismantling

Early extra buildings cost Metal only. The first shop costs no Machine Parts because it must be capable of making the first Parts. Advanced buildings/upgrades use both Metal and Parts. Repair continues using Supplies. LiquidFuel/Oxidizer are operating propellants, not a construction currency.

The player can use locally produced materials or optionally import them from another colony. Local production is sufficient for startup and basic expansion; never force importing a construction kit just to begin. Campaign contracts unlock blueprints and remain the main progression quest giver; resource production pays the cost of applying those blueprints.

Do not dismantle a machine being repaired, a terminal with occupied vehicle/handling jobs, or storage whose removal leaves inventory plus reservations above remaining capacity. Salvage itself requires destination storage room before commitment. A building with an unpaid/unfinished construction job follows cancellation rules rather than receiving completed-building salvage. Capacity changes, demolition and salvage occur once at command UT; repeated clicks/reload cannot duplicate materials.

Relocation disables routes, resolves/export/discards inventory with explicit confirmation, and packs remaining starter pieces back into the hab with their saved tier/condition. Other equipment must be salvaged or left behind explicitly; starter-kit entitlements never reset. Ground depletion stays. A new site receives a new colony identity but references the same hab's remaining kit receipt. Orbital depots instead retain their core's built-in starter capabilities; extra modules follow material construction and physical mass accounting.

### 3.6 Colony footprint and expansion

The 24 buildable cells are an intentional v1 limit. No extra deck or merged colony inventory in this release. Upgrade storage/machines in place, dismantle unneeded equipment safely, or launch another hab and connect its separate ledger through normal surface/rocket services. Nearby colonies share geographic deposits where applicable, not storage, crew, power or free transfers. Instantaneous local transfers mean transfers within one endpoint only.

The grid UI shows used/free cells and the footprint of queued jobs. Before starting a build, show the remaining cells and whether the endpoint still has space for the player's selected next facilities; this is advisory, not an invented mandatory layout. Provide a compact starter-layout example that fits all nine tokens plus expansion storage, a shop and power. Packed starter pieces remain recoverable under section 3.4. Never silently expand the deck when storage is full.

