## 6 Processing and crew

Recipes run independently per building. A hab has one basic smelter. A shop selects machine or pack mode; it cannot run both simultaneously.

| Recipe | Inputs per second | Outputs per second |
|---|---|---|
| Smelt | 0.50 batch ore; 12 colony power * origin hardness | Metal = 0.50 * grade * origin yield; Slag = 0.50 - Metal |
| Machine | 0.05 Metal; 8 colony power | 0.02 Parts |
| Pack | 0.02 Metal; 4 colony power | 0.05 Supplies |

Apply one crew factor and one RateMultiplier to nominal rates, exactly once. Stored ore uses its origin profile; moving ore to Kerbin cannot rewrite its yield/hardness. Slag is dumped beyond capacity by default; player can disable dumping. When enabled, no storage space for slag does not stall the recipe. Machine/Pack are abstractions, not mass-conserving chemistry; do not falsely describe them as conserving mass. Smelting conserves ore/metal/slag mass.

Crew factors: no crew 0; crew but no Engineer 0.50; Engineer levels 0..5 = 0.60,0.75,0.90,1.00,1.15,1.30. Highest Engineer on the anchor vessel applies. Repairs require an Engineer. These factors deliberately replace the old contradictory table. Persist crew snapshots for scene gaps and update after advancing to the crew-change timestamp. Missing unloaded crew data uses the last verified snapshot and logs once; do not interpret API failure as crew death.

RecipeMath aggregates duplicate input and output resource rates before computing limits. Inputs and non-dump outputs limit the entire recipe. Return actual active time, deltas, dumped amounts, and final limiting reasons. Shortage flags describe constraints at the final limit, not every constraint encountered while iterating. No input/output leg with zero rate creates invalid division. Pure math must not mutate caller pools.

## 7 Reliable colony power

### 7.1 Explicit owned system

Every deployed hab includes a colony battery (2000 power units) and a starter generator (20 power units/s). These are integrated ledger components with cosmetic visuals, not stock vessel generators. The starter generator is a deliberate gameplay abstraction with no carried fuel requirement. The UI labels colony power separately from spacecraft EC.

Factories, drills, and shipment departures use this colony battery and generation. Do not call RequestResource for colony power. Do not add stock panel output into this balance. This ensures one owner computes loaded and unloaded power and avoids competing background resource simulators.

Building options: baseline basic battery bank adds 2000 capacity and costs 60 Metal with no Parts or campaign requirement; advanced battery bank adds 8000 capacity and uses the c03 blueprint and section 17 recipe; solar array rated 40 units/s at Kerbin's solar distance; generator upgrade raises starter output to 40 units/s; advanced generator produces 120 units/s. Continuous generators are abstract industrial utilities maintained through funds and maintenance, not delivered fuel. No perpetual claim about real reactor fuel is needed.

### 7.1.1 Early surface transport reachability

Retain full-cycle electric prepayment from the actual source battery; do not invent off-ledger energy storage. Basic battery banks are baseline buildings, separate from the advanced c03 reward, and receive no free charge on construction. Added empty capacity charges from owned generation. This lets local Metal production solve an early transport-energy shortage before Parts exist.

Deployment/prospecting preview includes great-circle distance to KSC, a basic electric quote for 100 ore units (1 t), full-cycle power requirement, current battery capacity, additional basic banks needed, build cost/cells and charging estimate. Show accessible-now, accessible-after-baseline-expansion or unsupported. The tutorial recommends a site within current range, but distance alone does not forbid a valid colony. Explain the Engineer repair dependency before deployment; no Engineer is a warning, not a new deployment restriction.

For the shipped baseline, 100 ore costs 200 transport power plus max(120,distance/80) power for empty return. With capacity 2000 the ideal maximum range is 144000 m, ignoring other committed demand. Freeze this independent fixture. Test farther sites becoming feasible after locally built banks, empty added capacity, power competition, deck limits and c02 access without advanced unlocks. Quotes show the maximum feasible load and full-cycle power instead of a generic fuel warning.

### 7.2 Simulation

For each interval integrate generation, machinery consumption, and battery charge together. Production can run from live generation with an empty battery. Never first run machines until the starting battery empties and only afterward apply generation. Distribute scarce power by persisted priority groups: maintenance, construction, ore drill, smelter, fuel-feed pump, refinery, shop; equal priority machines share proportionally to requested draw. The player may change priorities in the UI.

Solar generation uses a documented sampled approximation: fixed absolute UT boundaries every 60 s, sample solar flux and geometric illumination at the interval midpoint; hold output constant within that interval. Compute body rotation/orbital position from verified celestial APIs for the requested UT, not the active camera's lighting. Include horizon night and parent-body occultation; local terrain shadows are not modeled. Inverse-square flux scales against Kerbin reference distance. Provide a pure geometry helper and tests. If the adapter cannot produce a valid environment, suspend solar contribution with a visible diagnostic; continuous generation still works.

Use the same samples whether loaded or unloaded. Never resample based on frame duration or warp. Record approximation in README and UI. Split battery integration at empty/full times and activity changes. Clamp battery to [0,capacity]. A full battery discards excess generation; it cannot grant shipment credit or goods.

### 7.3 Acceptance

- A starter colony mines and smelts while unloaded without depending on spacecraft EC.
- Empty battery with adequate continuous generation still supports production.
- One hour loaded and one hour unloaded with the same snapshots have equivalent final inventory/power within declared numeric tolerance.
- Day/night solar samples occur on identical UT boundaries under different tick partitions.
- Factory consumption cannot also reduce stock ElectricCharge.

## 8 Maintenance and faults

Each drill, smelter, fuel-feed pump, refinery, shop, and continuous generator has condition [0,1]. Passive storage/batteries, terminals and transport hulls do not have condition wear in v1; do not invent an additional fleet maintenance system. Wear applies only to actual operating seconds; base wear 0.000002/s, configurable per building. Stop exactly at condition zero, including during long catch-up; do not apply a long productive step and discover afterward that it should have failed halfway through.

Default mode supports automatic preventive maintenance once its service blueprint is unlocked at c05. Before c05, manual Supply-based service and the emergency Service Visit are available; an enabled AutoMaintenance preference does not bypass the blueprint gate. At condition <=0.35, a building with an Engineer, 20 local Supplies, and a functioning maintenance service begins a 60 UT-second service. Reserve Supplies once at start; the building pauses during service. Service proceeds loaded or unloaded and across warp, consumes its reservation at completion, clears faults, and restores condition to 1. Persist service state and remaining duration. If no supplies exist, continue until a fault/zero condition stops it and show the shortage. Disabling automatic maintenance permits manual start of the same job.

The hab has an emergency Service Visit option costing 500 funds in career, or zero funds in other modes, with a 60 UT-second service time. It requires an Engineer but no Supplies. This prevents a first-colony deadlock where the shop needed to produce Supplies is itself broken. It services only the selected building and never adds inventory. Repeated jobs must charge once each. Supply-based maintenance remains preferable for established networks.

Optional Hard Maintenance mode makes jobs advance only while loaded at warp <=4x. Persist pause state and show why. Default campaign does not require repeatedly visiting colonies to wait through repair timers. Crew departure pauses service; reservation remains. Cancel returns reserved Supplies, refunds no charged service-visit funds, and resets job state.

Fault occurrence is deterministic: once per new 3600 s UT window for machines that actually operated during the preceding window, threshold = max(0,0.50-condition). Use hash(buildingId,window,"occurrence") for occurrence. Use a separate hash(buildingId,window,"type") for jam/wear/short with equal thirds. Jam stops; wear multiplies effective rates by 0.5; short triples power demand only. Faults do not stack. All three types must be reachable. Persist last evaluated window; machines off for years must not roll millions of idle faults.

Difficulty options: wear multiplier, faults enabled, auto maintenance enabled, hard maintenance. No crew death or stock part failure system.

### 8.1 Maintenance pacing acceptance

At wear multiplier 1, uninterrupted operation reaches condition zero after 500000 s (about 23.15 Kerbin days); preventive threshold 0.35 occurs after 325000 operating seconds. These are initial game-balance values, not verified pacing. Wear uses actual operating time, so outages/services do not advance it. c05 still gates automatic preventive maintenance; before that, manual Supply service and the paid emergency visit remain available.

The starter guidance recommends an Engineer and shows repair eligibility. A crew-only colony may operate at its stated efficiency, but cannot be presented as repair-self-sufficient. Engineer absence pauses repair with an actionable message; never generate an Engineer or convert API failure into crew loss.

Calibrate c01-c05 using feasible flight windows and the starter production rates. Record service visits per campaign stage, forced colony switches, productive time lost and Supplies demand. Default service proceeds in background; no colony visit is required merely to watch a timer. Target zero mandatory scene visits solely for service timers and no mandatory emergency repair before the near-KSC c02 reference completion. Persistent early repair repetition requires an explicit balance amendment, not undocumented adapter changes.

## 9 Fuel production and transport infrastructure

### 9.1 Propellant resources and factories

Use LiquidFuel plus Oxidizer for every rocket template in this release. Match the stock 0.9:1.1 unit consumption ratio; with stock equal densities this is 45% LiquidFuel and 55% Oxidizer by mass. Validate densities at startup and compute mixture mass from both; never assume one resource's units equal tonnes. Do not add hydrogen, methane, boil-off, or nuclear-only fuel modes yet. This is KSP-consistent fuel accounting, not chemical modeling of actual regolith.

The basic refinery and fuel-feed pump are baseline buildings, available before the first off-world shipment. The first of each is included in the starter kit; extras are built from local Metal. Fuel is never granted by placing a building. A new endpoint must actually produce, receive, or explicitly buy local fuel before its transports can depart.

The refinery has two mutually exclusive modes:

- Ore ISRU consumes 0.25 units/s of Lodeworks graded ore plus 15 colony power/s. At default stock densities, produces 0.18 LiquidFuel/s, 0.22 Oxidizer/s, and 0.05 Slag/s. 80% of input mass becomes mixture; 20% becomes slag. Grade affects Metal smelting, not fuel yield. Consumed ore loses campaign eligibility and cannot be counted as returned ore. Origin still appears in production diagnostics.
- Fuel-feed mode consumes 0.50 feed units/s plus 10 power/s. At default densities, produces the same fuel/oxidizer/slag outputs as above. No direct creation of fuel from electricity alone.

Generalize rates from target mixture mass and runtime densities so changed metadata cannot create mass. Both outputs must have storage room; unlike Slag, neither fuel nor oxidizer is dumped by default. Production stalls if either would overflow. Crew, wear, repair, priority and background integration apply to the pump and refinery. Implement the recipe through the same resource math as the other factories.

Pump output is 0.50 feed units/s at full reservoir and crew factor 1, using 6 power/s before crew. It taps an independent finite fuel-feed reservoir in the same geographic cell, not the metal-ore vein. Reservoir capacity = 20000 * volatileFactor units. Factors: Kerbin 2.0, Mun 0.5, Minmus 2.0, Duna 1.5, Ike 0.5, Dres 0.5, Laythe 2.0, Vall 1.5, Tylo 0.25, Bop 0.5, Pol 1.0, Eeloo 2.0, Eve 1.0, Gilly 0.25, unknown 0.5. These are configurable game abstractions; label the deposit Fuel feed, not a claim that raw finished rocket fuel lies underground. Apply the same analytic declining extraction/residual rule as ore, with no stock abundance query required. Multiple pumps share reservoir depletion.

Base colony integrated and dedicated storage follows section 4.2. LiquidFuel and Oxidizer require separate tank placeables; building one does not add capacity to the other or to feed/ore/products. Display available mixture, individual components, and unusable imbalance. Allow fuel as cargo, either a paired mixture manifest or individual component manifest; paired shipments use the 0.9:1.1 ratio. Carrying fuel as cargo consumes payload mass in addition to propulsion fuel.

Keep a refinery's reserve policy: do not consume ore earmarked for accepted export quotas or below a player-set ore reserve. Player can explicitly override this. No hidden infinite fuel reservoir or automatic remote purchase.

### 9.2 Endpoints and vehicle location

Endpoint kinds: surface colony, registered orbital depot, and ksc-receiver. Same-body surface service is a configurable electrically powered ground/drone abstraction and consumes power; any surface-to-orbit, orbit-to-surface, or cross-body route requires rocket propellant. Do not allow a ground drone to cross bodies or reach a station.

Surface colonies require an enabled terminal. Depots require enabled cargo handling and berths. KSC has both inherently. Arrival to KSC consumes accepted ore/products for quest delivery; fuel cargo to KSC can be explicitly sold at stock metadata price after fees, with a preview, and does not earn an ore objective. A transport at KSC may buy its own next-leg fuel using actual funds; surface/depot remote fueling still requires local inventory or delivered fuel. Sandbox purchases are free at KSC only and are labeled local supply, not a physics exception throughout the network.

Replace the old return-time-only slot with a real scenario-owned TransportVehicle record: id, templateId, homeEndpointId, currentEndpointId or transitId, cargo capacity, tank capacity, onboard LiquidFuel/Oxidizer, state, assigned route, paid hull cost, and availability UT. Cosmetic appearance does not define vehicle capabilities. A vehicle arrives physically in the ledger at its destination; it cannot simultaneously serve its origin or teleport home.

Loading and unloading consume 0.5 colony power per cargo unit for rocket services, while the electric surface service uses its own table. Handling funds fee is 25 + 10*cargoMassT per rocket departure, and 25 + 0.2*cargoUnits per electric departure, charged once. These small local service fees do not replace actual fuel debits. KSC handling power is supplied locally with its service fee; a depot/colony needs its own power. Default surface terminal handling throughput is 1 t/min, upgraded 4 t/min, with one simultaneous loader; stage manifests before flight without delivering them prematurely. A cancelled loading job restores unburned reserved fuel/cargo within the transaction, but does not refund completed handling fees.

Vehicles are commissioned through a terminal/depot hangar. Surface commissioning costs funds, represents local fabrication/service, requires no imported building materials, and takes 600 UT seconds to add an empty vehicle. Orbital commissioning instead orders a dry hull from a selected surface terminal; ship that hull to the depot as transportHull cargo with mass equal to the template dry mass, paying and consuming the actual ascent/transfer fuel. Arrival plus 600 UT seconds of setup converts the delivered hull into the located vehicle without changing total mass. Do not manufacture a dry tug/capsule magically in orbit for a funds-only fee. The hull order is paid once and retains its ID through delivery; the same hull cannot become two vehicles. This is automated transport hardware supply, not a required stack of Metal/Parts construction resources. The commissioning UI may arrange its hull-delivery route, but must show the costs/wait and require a feasible existing surface carrier. Never mint propellant with a hull. Retiring a vehicle returns its remaining fuel to available local capacity and refunds 50% of hull funds paid, once. If insufficient storage exists, refuse until fuel is transferred or explicitly discarded. Depot berth/hangar limits bound the fleet; one transport cannot be commissioned repeatedly with one paid receipt.

Player route policy selects one-way relocation or a repeatable cycle with actual outbound/return edges. A repeatable vehicle is available at its home only after the return flight arrives and local turnaround is complete. Empty return flights require their own fuel budget. Fuel can be reserved at the destination before departure, or carried on board for return, subject to tank/mass limits. If neither is possible, block a repeat-cycle departure. A one-way relocation may proceed without a return plan, but show its new location and remaining fuel.

### 9.3 Initial templates

These configurable abstract craft profiles are starting balance values, not proof that a rendered cube could fly. The templates provide engine/tank/hull assumptions the planner can inspect. A template cannot traverse an edge outside its allowed role or range. transportHull is an internal cargo kind with immutable templateId and per-hull mass, not an ore/product resource; it can neither satisfy a resource quest nor be converted to propellant. Cargo capacity also limits hull delivery, so a light shuttle cannot carry a heavy tug whose dry mass exceeds its payload.

