For c05, newly produced Parts need eligible production lot records for that contract; exporting pre-existing Parts cannot satisfy the delivery leg. Treat these as internal eligibility sublots within the same Parts ledger, not a new resource. Consume oldest sublots deterministically and move tags with shipments. Document this narrower provenance requirement and test it. c06 similarly tags Supplies produced after acceptance. Other commodity contracts must declare whether production provenance is required.

Quantities must be checked against low and typical deposit capacities, including ore consumed to fuel the network. Main c03 permits a direct early shuttle delivery if feasible; d01/d02 teach and then require a functioning depot network before c06. Do not require a still-locked capsule in d02: c03 unlocks it first. A d01 fuel delivery must contain fuel as cargo unloaded into the depot, not the vehicle onboard propellant already counted in its burns. d02 binds named depot IDs at acceptance and uses the consignment handoff history; touching a depot icon or rewriting an itinerary cannot satisfy it. A commercial fuel sale must consume fuel at the named endpoint and cannot repeatedly circulate the same mixture for reward.

Quantities must be checked against low and typical deposit capacities. The listed values are initial campaign balance, not proof of one-site feasibility. Multiple colonies are permitted, but never conceal a multi-site requirement; show expected amount and deposit estimates. Main quests have no deadline because fuel production, departure windows, long interplanetary trips and finite deposits should not create impossible offers.

Baseline capabilities exist before c01: basic hab, its smelter/generator/battery, exactly one complete included starter set, basic dedicated-storage recipes, and manual emergency service. First shop uses Metal only. All production needed to pay for extra basic buildings is available without Machine Parts. c01 unlocks the surface service needed for c02. Early rockets/depots/tugs unlock together at c02 so the player can bootstrap a genuine fueled network without needing its own later output. Ordinary stock research prerequisites may delay access to the basic hab but must not require a Lodeworks quest that needs that hab. No blueprint requires its own output or capability to obtain itself.

### 11.3 Parts and unlock enforcement

Campaign parts are original Lodeworks hab variants using referenced stock model paths until original art exists. All may establish a basic colony; variants improve built-in equipment without requiring a new resource catalog:

- lw-hab-250: baseline; 2000 battery, 20 generation, base capacities.
- lw-hab-remote: 4000 battery and integrated basic transport terminal, but no free vehicles or fuel.
- lw-hab-heavy: 8000 battery, twice the baseline per-resource integrated buffer capacities, and two integrated vehicle hangar capacities, but no free vehicles or fuel. It receives one normal starter set, not multiple.
- lw-hab-survey: remote capabilities plus richer on-site prospecting display; it does not reveal unvisited deposits globally.
- lw-hab-endurance: heavy capabilities plus 40 continuous generation and built-in preventive service.

Use one explicit blueprint registry mapping campaign rewards to part internal names, building kinds, upgrades, and service capabilities. No arbitrary stock engine or tank is locked or patched. Future rewards can add original parts through configuration.

Before implementing the full campaign, prove a public-API adapter can keep unrewarded Lodeworks parts unavailable in the editor/R&D while enabling them per save after reward. Verify AvailablePart, ResearchAndDevelopment, purchase/tech state, editor refresh, and stock save behavior against installed assemblies. Do not assume setting a UI flag alone enforces a lock. The adapter must prevent ordinary entry-cost purchase from bypassing campaign requirements, and respect any stock science prerequisite. Granting the blueprint permanently satisfies the campaign requirement; no duplicate entry payment is required for the awarded Lodeworks part, but unmet stock tech still needs research.

Prototype tests: locked part cannot be selected/bought; reward enables it; switching to another save restores that save's locks; sandbox has all parts; existing vessels with locked parts still load; an imported saved craft containing a locked part cannot be launched as a bypass in career (use a verified editor/launch validation API). Do not alter shared part prefab state or other saves. If public APIs cannot meet these conditions without Harmony, mark this feasibility phase blocked with observed signatures and the failing behavior. Do not replace actual part unlocks with a cosmetic badge and claim completion.

### 11.4 Commercial contracts

After c05, offer optional Metal and Parts orders delivered to KSC, and supply deliveries to named existing colonies. Reward funds and reputation, not mandatory progression blueprints. Select reachable existing endpoints and available service tiers. Deadlines must be at least known earliest round-trip/production estimate plus 10 Kerbin days; show assumptions. Never offer Sun/Jool surface targets. Cancel/fail cannot undo campaign blueprints or delete unrelated shipments. Failure funds = reward/5 and reputation penalty 2, applied once through verified stock contract mechanisms.

## 12 Simulation ordering and time

WorldSimulator.Advance(targetUT) is the sole clock owner. It advances colony extraction, processing, power, maintenance, shipments, vehicle arrivals/turnaround, routes, fuel manufacture, vehicle loading/burns, refuels, depot handoffs, orbital environment updates and campaign events in chronological order. Loaded, packed, unloaded, space-center, and tracking-station views use the same function. Update displayed state before saving or evaluating a contract at its timestamp. No factories tick in the editor; the editor may read campaign unlocks only.

Use event-based integration with shared intervals. Interval boundaries include construction/upgrade completion, launch windows, burn events, refuel/handling completion, vehicle ready times, shipment arrivals, slot ready times, service completion, wear zero, fault windows, solar samples, route scheduling boundaries, batch exhaustion, deposit threshold, storage full/empty, and power full/empty. Inputs changing during a gap must affect the remaining gap. A cargo arrival at t=100 can feed a shop between t=100 and targetUT; it cannot arrive only after all production has finished.

At equal timestamps use this order: integrate the previous open interval; commit completions and arrivals; apply maintenance/fault transitions; apply player commands at that timestamp; evaluate route departures; evaluate campaign completion/rewards. Sort independent equal-time entities by stable ID. Do not run each converter through the full catch-up gap sequentially. Inventory/power sharing is integrated over the same interval, with simultaneous rates and resource limits; deterministic priorities resolve competition. RecipeMath remains the shared limiter, but a single converter Step is not a substitute for colony/world scheduling.

Tests must prove partition invariance for shared power, drill-to-smelter-to-shop chains, a full output later drained by another building, mid-gap shipments, fuel burn/refuel events, window opening, depot handoffs, faults, maintenance, and routes. Compare Advance(T) against arbitrary partitions to T. Commands/environment snapshots occur at the same absolute timestamps in both tests. Allow absolute 1e-7 units plus relative 1e-9 for long runs. Determinism means these defined rules, not exact real KSP physics.

Catch-up complexity is O(events and fixed environment samples), not O(elapsed seconds). No claim of O(1) when processing fault/solar/route boundaries. Skip idle spans analytically. Set a per-frame processing budget (default 4 ms), persist a targetUT cursor, show Catching up, and continue on later frames. Never discard unprocessed elapsed time or advance lastSimUT past it. Disable mutating UI commands for entities not yet caught up. Save intermediate state with its actual simulation cursor; resume after reload. Do not evaluate completion/rewards against stale world time.

Use UT, not DateTime or Unity delta time, for all gameplay totals. If UT rolls back, restore the loaded save's state and cursor; do not retain newer singleton state. Corrupt negative clock gaps reset the affected cursor with diagnostic. Excessive future gaps are processed incrementally rather than pretending skipped time was simulated. Settings may cap accepted corrupt targets, but the warning and unapplied interval must remain explicit.

